    "intro": "Each year on March 7, Guide Catholic highlights Sts. Perpetua and Felicity, remembered as a martyr of the Catholic Church from Carthage, North Africa (d. 203). Perpetua kept a prison diary, one of the earliest Christian texts by a woman. That detail is why Sts. Perpetua still appears in parish bulletins and school religion lessons around March 7. Martyrs of Carthage Whose Diary Survives — that is the shorthand families and RCIA teams use for Sts. Perpetua. As patron of mothers, martyrs, and North Africa, Sts. Perpetua is asked for intercession in needs that match that patronage; the sections below unpack history, vocation, and practical observance for March 7.",
    "earlyLife": "To understand Sts. Perpetua, start with place and time: Carthage, North Africa, d. 203. Felicity gave birth in prison hours before both were sent to the arena. That detail is why Sts. Perpetua still appears in parish bulletins and school religion lessons around March 7. Not every anecdote about Sts. Perpetua is equally documented, yet the core virtues the Church praises are stable enough for feast-day preaching. Knowing that landscape — language, rulers, poverty or privilege in Carthage, North Africa — keeps Sts. Perpetua from becoming a floating icon detached from real cost.",
    "vocationAndMinistry": "Sts. Perpetua's vocation climaxed in blood witness when fidelity to Christ became a public crime. Martyred in Carthage during persecution under Septimius Severus. That detail is why Sts. Perpetua still appears in parish bulletins and school religion lessons around March 7. The pattern behind the headlines is familiar: sacraments, repentance, charity, and courage repeated until death. Anyone discerning a call connected to mothers can ask: which virtue of Sts. Perpetua do I lack most this week?.",
    "historicalContext": "Their Passion account is read annually in the Liturgy of the Hours. That detail is why Sts. Perpetua still appears in parish bulletins and school religion lessons around March 7. March 7 is how the Roman calendar refuses to let Sts. Perpetua become a private hobby of specialists. When Catholics on different continents open the same day's calendar entry for Sts. Perpetua, the communion of saints becomes practical, not theoretical.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Catholics ask Sts. Perpetua to pray with them because heaven is not a retirement from the Body of Christ. As patron of mothers, martyrs, and North Africa, Sts. Perpetua is a natural companion for novenas, parish festivals, and quiet kitchen-table intentions that match those causes. Where shrines or relics of Sts. Perpetua exist, pilgrims still arrive looking for conversion as much as consolation. On March 7, even a short visit to a statue or image of Sts. Perpetua can become a deliberate act of communion rather than nostalgia.",
    "patronages": "Sts. Perpetua and Felicity is especially invoked by people whose lives touch mothers, martyrs, and North Africa. Patronage names a friendship in heaven, not a superstition that bypasses the Cross. A simple March 7 practice: name one intention aloud, pray an Our Father and Hail Mary, then do one work of mercy that mirrors Sts. Perpetua's charity. Parishes named for Sts. Perpetua can treat this date as a soft “patronal day” even when it is only a memorial.",
    "legacy": "The lasting imprint of Sts. Perpetua is pastoral: a finished race that now encourages others still on the road. Parents can treat March 7 as a micro-feast: one story, one prayer, one act of kindness. Guide Catholic keeps this profile online so English-speaking Catholics can find Sts. Perpetua quickly when March 7 arrives — or when a need connected to mothers, martyrs, and North Africa suddenly becomes personal.",
    "howToHonorToday": "Make Mass the centerpiece of March 7 when possible — the Eucharist is how Catholics most fittingly honor Sts. Perpetua. At home, read one short paragraph about Sts. Perpetua and pray for someone whose struggle relates to mothers, martyrs, and North Africa. Choose a single act that Sts. Perpetua would recognize — mercy toward the poor, defense of truth, or hidden fidelity. If church is impossible that day, read Sts. Perpetua in the Roman Martyrology or a trusted Catholic reference, then make an act of spiritual communion and return to the sacraments when you can.",
