    "vocationAndMinistry": "St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen's vocation climaxed in blood witness when fidelity to Christ became a public crime. First martyr of the Congregation for the Propagation of the Faith. Catechists often open a short talk on April 24 with this episode from St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen's story. The pattern behind the headlines is familiar: sacraments, repentance, charity, and courage repeated until death. Anyone discerning a call connected to lawyers can ask: which virtue of St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen do I lack most this week?.",
    "historicalContext": "Canonized in 1746 by Pope Benedict XIV. Catechists often open a short talk on April 24 with this episode from St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen's story. April 24 is how the Roman calendar refuses to let St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen become a private hobby of specialists. When Catholics on different continents open the same day's calendar entry for St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen, the communion of saints becomes practical, not theoretical.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Catholics ask St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen to pray with them because heaven is not a retirement from the Body of Christ. As patron of lawyers, missionaries, and Capuchins, St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen is a natural companion for novenas, parish festivals, and quiet kitchen-table intentions that match those causes. Where shrines or relics of St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen exist, pilgrims still arrive looking for conversion as much as consolation. On April 24, even a short visit to a statue or image of St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen can become a deliberate act of communion rather than nostalgia.",
    "patronages": "St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen is especially invoked by people whose lives touch lawyers, missionaries, and Capuchins. Patronage names a friendship in heaven, not a superstition that bypasses the Cross. A simple April 24 practice: name one intention aloud, pray an Our Father and Hail Mary, then do one work of mercy that mirrors St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen's charity. Parishes named for St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen can treat this date as a soft “patronal day” even when it is only a memorial.",
    "legacy": "The lasting imprint of St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen is pastoral: a finished race that now encourages others still on the road. Parents can treat April 24 as a micro-feast: one story, one prayer, one act of kindness. Guide Catholic keeps this profile online so English-speaking Catholics can find St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen quickly when April 24 arrives — or when a need connected to lawyers, missionaries, and Capuchins suddenly becomes personal.",
    "howToHonorToday": "Make Mass the centerpiece of April 24 when possible — the Eucharist is how Catholics most fittingly honor St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen. At home, read one short paragraph about St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen and pray for someone whose struggle relates to lawyers, missionaries, and Capuchins. Choose a single act that St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen would recognize — mercy toward the poor, defense of truth, or hidden fidelity. If church is impossible that day, read St. Fidelis of Sigmaringen in the Roman Martyrology or a trusted Catholic reference, then make an act of spiritual communion and return to the sacraments when you can.",
