    "vocationAndMinistry": "St. Romuald's vocation unfolded in preaching, pastoral care, and perseverance under long responsibility rather than a single dramatic hour. Reformed monasteries across Italy and sent missionaries to Hungary. Catechists often open a short talk on June 19 with this episode from St. Romuald's story. The pattern behind the headlines is familiar: sacraments, repentance, charity, and courage repeated until death. Anyone discerning a call connected to Camaldolese can ask: which virtue of St. Romuald do I lack most this week?.",
    "historicalContext": "His rule balances solitude and community in monastic life. Catechists often open a short talk on June 19 with this episode from St. Romuald's story. June 19 is how the Roman calendar refuses to let St. Romuald become a private hobby of specialists. When Catholics on different continents open the same day's calendar entry for St. Romuald, the communion of saints becomes practical, not theoretical.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Catholics ask St. Romuald to pray with them because heaven is not a retirement from the Body of Christ. As patron of Camaldolese, monks, and reformers, St. Romuald is a natural companion for novenas, parish festivals, and quiet kitchen-table intentions that match those causes. Where shrines or relics of St. Romuald exist, pilgrims still arrive looking for conversion as much as consolation. On June 19, even a short visit to a statue or image of St. Romuald can become a deliberate act of communion rather than nostalgia.",
    "patronages": "St. Romuald is especially invoked by people whose lives touch Camaldolese, monks, and reformers. Patronage names a friendship in heaven, not a superstition that bypasses the Cross. A simple June 19 practice: name one intention aloud, pray an Our Father and Hail Mary, then do one work of mercy that mirrors St. Romuald's charity. Parishes named for St. Romuald can treat this date as a soft “patronal day” even when it is only a memorial.",
    "legacy": "The lasting imprint of St. Romuald is pastoral: a finished race that now encourages others still on the road. Parents can treat June 19 as a micro-feast: one story, one prayer, one act of kindness. Guide Catholic keeps this profile online so English-speaking Catholics can find St. Romuald quickly when June 19 arrives — or when a need connected to Camaldolese, monks, and reformers suddenly becomes personal.",
