    "intro": "July 15 belongs to St. Bonaventure on the Catholic calendar — a Doctor of the Church whose teaching still guides Catholic theology formed in Bagnoregio, Italy during 1221–1274. Franciscan theologian and Doctor of the Church called the Seraphic Doctor. Catechists often open a short talk on July 15 with this episode from St. Bonaventure's story. If you only remember one line about St. Bonaventure, remember this: Seraphic Doctor of the Franciscan Order. As patron of Bologna, theologians, and Franciscans, St. Bonaventure is asked for intercession in needs that match that patronage; the sections below unpack history, vocation, and practical observance for July 15.",
    "earlyLife": "St. Bonaventure's story is rooted in Bagnoregio, Italy, across the years 1221–1274. General of the Franciscan order who defended its charism at councils. Catechists often open a short talk on July 15 with this episode from St. Bonaventure's story. Sources mix chronicles, liturgical memory, and later devotion; the Church still presents St. Bonaventure as a model of holiness even when minor anecdotes remain debated. Knowing that landscape — language, rulers, poverty or privilege in Bagnoregio, Italy — keeps St. Bonaventure from becoming a floating icon detached from real cost.",
    "vocationAndMinistry": "St. Bonaventure's vocation was intellectual charity — writing and teaching so the Church could confess the faith without confusion. Wrote The Mind's Journey to God, a classic of mystical theology. Catechists often open a short talk on July 15 with this episode from St. Bonaventure's story. What the calendar preserves is not celebrity but a repeatable Christian shape — worship of God and mercy toward people. Anyone discerning a call connected to Bologna can ask: which virtue of St. Bonaventure do I lack most this week?.",
    "historicalContext": "Cardinal Bishop of Albano; died at the Council of Lyon in 1274. Catechists often open a short talk on July 15 with this episode from St. Bonaventure's story. Fixing St. Bonaventure on July 15 synchronizes memory worldwide: the same date for missals, schools, and family prayer. In a noisy news cycle, a fixed feast for St. Bonaventure is a small act of Christian timekeeping.",
