    "intro": "Each year on July 28, Guide Catholic highlights St. Innocent I, remembered as a confessor whose steadfast preaching and service shaped local churches from Albano, Italy (d. 417). Pope who asserted Roman primacy over the entire Church. Readers searching for St. Innocent I usually want this concrete memory, not a vague slogan. Pope Who Defined the Biblical Canon — that is the shorthand families and RCIA teams use for St. Innocent I. As patron of popes, Rome, and canon law, St. Innocent I is asked for intercession in needs that match that patronage; the sections below unpack history, vocation, and practical observance for July 28.",
    "earlyLife": "To understand St. Innocent I, start with place and time: Albano, Italy, d. 417. Confirmed the canonical books of Scripture for the Western Church. Readers searching for St. Innocent I usually want this concrete memory, not a vague slogan. Not every anecdote about St. Innocent I is equally documented, yet the core virtues the Church praises are stable enough for feast-day preaching. Knowing that landscape — language, rulers, poverty or privilege in Albano, Italy — keeps St. Innocent I from becoming a floating icon detached from real cost.",
