    "earlyLife": "To understand St. Helena, start with place and time: Drepanum, Bithynia, c. 250–330. Built churches at Bethlehem, the Mount of Olives, and Golgotha. It is the sort of specific witness that separates St. Helena from generic “holy person” summaries. Not every anecdote about St. Helena is equally documented, yet the core virtues the Church praises are stable enough for feast-day preaching. Knowing that landscape — language, rulers, poverty or privilege in Drepanum, Bithynia — keeps St. Helena from becoming a floating icon detached from real cost.",
    "vocationAndMinistry": "St. Helena's vocation unfolded in preaching, pastoral care, and perseverance under long responsibility rather than a single dramatic hour. Patroness of archaeologists and those in difficult marriages. It is the sort of specific witness that separates St. Helena from generic “holy person” summaries. The pattern behind the headlines is familiar: sacraments, repentance, charity, and courage repeated until death. Anyone discerning a call connected to archaeologists can ask: which virtue of St. Helena do I lack most this week?.",
    "historicalContext": "Venerated in both East and West for her pilgrimage to the Holy Land. It is the sort of specific witness that separates St. Helena from generic “holy person” summaries. August 18 is how the Roman calendar refuses to let St. Helena become a private hobby of specialists. When Catholics on different continents open the same day's calendar entry for St. Helena, the communion of saints becomes practical, not theoretical.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Catholics ask St. Helena to pray with them because heaven is not a retirement from the Body of Christ. As patron of archaeologists, converts, and difficult marriages, St. Helena is a natural companion for novenas, parish festivals, and quiet kitchen-table intentions that match those causes. Where shrines or relics of St. Helena exist, pilgrims still arrive looking for conversion as much as consolation. On August 18, even a short visit to a statue or image of St. Helena can become a deliberate act of communion rather than nostalgia.",
