    "patronages": "St. Teresa of Calcutta is especially invoked by people whose lives touch world youth, the unwanted, and Missionaries of Charity. Catholic teaching is clear: saints intercede; they do not replace the mediation of Christ. A simple September 5 practice: name one intention aloud, pray an Our Father and Hail Mary, then do one work of mercy that mirrors St. Teresa of Calcutta's charity. If St. Teresa of Calcutta is your confirmation name, September 5 is a yearly checkup on the promises that name implies.",
    "legacy": "St. Teresa of Calcutta survives in missals, church titles, and art because concrete holiness still draws people tired of cynicism. Homilists who name St. Teresa of Calcutta when the calendar aligns give the faithful a person, not only a principle. Guide Catholic keeps this profile online so English-speaking Catholics can find St. Teresa of Calcutta quickly when September 5 arrives — or when a need connected to world youth, the unwanted, and Missionaries of Charity suddenly becomes personal.",
    "howToHonorToday": "If your schedule allows, attend Mass on September 5; even a quiet weekday memorial joins you to the universal Church remembering St. Teresa of Calcutta. At home, read one short paragraph about St. Teresa of Calcutta and pray for someone whose struggle relates to world youth, the unwanted, and Missionaries of Charity. Add one concrete deed: a donation, a visit, a decade of the Rosary, or a message of reconciliation offered in St. Teresa of Calcutta's spirit. If church is impossible that day, read St. Teresa of Calcutta in the Roman Martyrology or a trusted Catholic reference, then make an act of spiritual communion and return to the sacraments when you can.",
