    "vocationAndMinistry": "St. Thérèse of Lisieux's vocation was intellectual charity — writing and teaching so the Church could confess the faith without confusion. Wrote Story of a Soul, one of the best-selling spiritual autobiographies. It is the sort of specific witness that separates St. Thérèse of Lisieux from generic “holy person” summaries. What the calendar preserves is not celebrity but a repeatable Christian shape — worship of God and mercy toward people. Anyone discerning a call connected to missions can ask: which virtue of St. Thérèse of Lisieux do I lack most this week?.",
    "historicalContext": "Patroness of missions despite never leaving her convent. It is the sort of specific witness that separates St. Thérèse of Lisieux from generic “holy person” summaries. Fixing St. Thérèse of Lisieux on October 1 synchronizes memory worldwide: the same date for missals, schools, and family prayer. In a noisy news cycle, a fixed feast for St. Thérèse of Lisieux is a small act of Christian timekeeping.",
