    "vocationAndMinistry": "St. John the Apostle's vocation was missionary: announcing Christ and ordering young churches where the Gospel was new. Only apostle not martyred; tradition holds he survived boiling in oil. It is the sort of specific witness that separates St. John the Apostle from generic “holy person” summaries. The pattern behind the headlines is familiar: sacraments, repentance, charity, and courage repeated until death. Anyone discerning a call connected to love can ask: which virtue of St. John the Apostle do I lack most this week?.",
    "historicalContext": "Patron of love, friendship, and theologians. It is the sort of specific witness that separates St. John the Apostle from generic “holy person” summaries. December 27 is how the Roman calendar refuses to let St. John the Apostle become a private hobby of specialists. When Catholics on different continents open the same day's calendar entry for St. John the Apostle, the communion of saints becomes practical, not theoretical.",
    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Catholics ask St. John the Apostle to pray with them because heaven is not a retirement from the Body of Christ. As patron of love, friendship, authors, and theologians, St. John the Apostle is a natural companion for novenas, parish festivals, and quiet kitchen-table intentions that match those causes. Where shrines or relics of St. John the Apostle exist, pilgrims still arrive looking for conversion as much as consolation. On December 27, even a short visit to a statue or image of St. John the Apostle can become a deliberate act of communion rather than nostalgia.",
    "patronages": "St. John the Apostle is especially invoked by people whose lives touch love, friendship, authors, and theologians. Patronage names a friendship in heaven, not a superstition that bypasses the Cross. A simple December 27 practice: name one intention aloud, pray an Our Father and Hail Mary, then do one work of mercy that mirrors St. John the Apostle's charity. Parishes named for St. John the Apostle can treat this date as a soft “patronal day” even when it is only a memorial.",
    "legacy": "The lasting imprint of St. John the Apostle is pastoral: a finished race that now encourages others still on the road. Parents can treat December 27 as a micro-feast: one story, one prayer, one act of kindness. Guide Catholic keeps this profile online so English-speaking Catholics can find St. John the Apostle quickly when December 27 arrives — or when a need connected to love, friendship, authors, and theologians suddenly becomes personal.",
    "howToHonorToday": "Make Mass the centerpiece of December 27 when possible — the Eucharist is how Catholics most fittingly honor St. John the Apostle. At home, read one short paragraph about St. John the Apostle and pray for someone whose struggle relates to love, friendship, authors, and theologians. Choose a single act that St. John the Apostle would recognize — mercy toward the poor, defense of truth, or hidden fidelity. If church is impossible that day, read St. John the Apostle in the Roman Martyrology or a trusted Catholic reference, then make an act of spiritual communion and return to the sacraments when you can.",
